# PROGRESSION ENGINE

Version 1.0

---

# Purpose

The Progression Engine determines how training evolves over time.

Its objective is not to increase load as quickly as possible.

Its objective is to maximize long-term adaptation.

Progression is earned.

Not scheduled.

---

# Mission

Every progression should improve performance while preserving recovery, movement quality and long-term consistency.

The engine continuously answers one question:

Is the athlete ready for a greater stimulus?

---

# Core Principle

Adaptation drives progression.

Completion alone does not.

The athlete progresses because adaptation has occurred.

Not because another week has passed.

---

# Philosophy

Progression is individualized.

Two athletes completing the same session may receive different progressions.

CAS evaluates adaptation,

not merely execution.

---

# Progressive Overload

CAS applies progressive overload conservatively.

Possible progression variables include:

Load

Volume

Intensity

Density

Exercise complexity

Velocity

Range of motion

Tempo

Rest duration

Exercise variation

Load is only one possible progression.

---

# Progression Hierarchy

When progression is appropriate,

CAS evaluates possible options in the following order:

Movement quality

↓

Training consistency

↓

Recovery

↓

Load

↓

Volume

↓

Exercise complexity

↓

Training density

Progression should never sacrifice technical quality.

---

# Inputs

The Progression Engine may consider:

Completed repetitions

Completed sets

Load achieved

RPE

RIR

Movement quality

Recovery estimate

Pain reports

Training consistency

Previous progression history

Fatigue trend

Athlete objective

Training phase

Competition schedule

Available equipment

Explainability confidence

---

# Progression Criteria

Progression should occur only when sufficient evidence exists.

Typical indicators include:

Repeated successful completion.

Stable recovery.

Acceptable effort.

No significant pain.

Technical consistency.

Adequate compliance.

No single variable should dominate.

---

# Progression Categories

Possible outcomes:

Increase stimulus

Maintain stimulus

Reduce stimulus

Modify stimulus

Replace stimulus

Progression is not always an increase.

---

# Increase Stimulus

Possible methods:

Increase load.

Increase repetitions.

Increase sets.

Reduce assistance.

Increase movement complexity.

Reduce rest.

Increase velocity demands.

Only one primary variable should change at a time.

---

# Maintain Stimulus

Maintenance is often the correct decision.

Reasons include:

Consolidating adaptation.

Improving movement quality.

Managing fatigue.

Preparing future progression.

Maintenance is not stagnation.

---

# Reduce Stimulus

Reasons include:

Poor recovery.

Repeated technical breakdown.

Pain.

Accumulated fatigue.

Illness.

Competition proximity.

Reducing stimulus protects long-term progress.

---

# Modify Stimulus

Sometimes progression changes quality rather than quantity.

Examples:

Front Squat

↓

Paused Front Squat

Heavy Bag

↓

Reactive Heavy Bag

Pull-up

↓

Explosive Pull-up

The objective changes while maintaining appropriate challenge.

---

# Deload

Deload is a progression strategy.

Not a failure.

Possible triggers:

Accumulated fatigue.

Reduced performance.

High monotony.

End of training block.

Competition preparation.

Deload should preserve movement quality while reducing stress.

---

# Plateau Detection

CAS continuously evaluates progression.

Possible plateau indicators:

Stable performance.

Repeated failures.

No adaptation.

Declining readiness.

Plateaus should trigger investigation,

not automatic load increases.

---

# Failure Analysis

Not every failed repetition requires regression.

CAS evaluates:

Technical error.

Fatigue.

Recovery.

Programming.

External stress.

Equipment issues.

Failure must be interpreted before action.

---

# Exercise-Specific Progression

Every exercise progresses differently.

Examples:

Maximal Strength

Small load increments.

Power

Velocity before load.

Hypertrophy

Volume before intensity.

Mobility

Range of motion.

Conditioning

Work density.

Heavy Bag

Technical quality before intensity.

The engine adapts progression to exercise purpose.

---

# Combat Sports

Combat athletes require progression beyond strength.

Examples:

Strike speed.

Strike power.

Reaction time.

Footwork.

Explosive transitions.

Round density.

Grip endurance.

Decision-making under fatigue.

Progression must remain sport-specific.

---

# Regression

Regression is sometimes appropriate.

Examples:

Pain.

Poor technique.

Extended interruption.

Injury.

Regression protects future adaptation.

It should never be presented as failure.

---

# Individual Baselines

Every athlete progresses differently.

CAS compares the athlete against:

Their own history.

Their own recovery.

Their own response.

Never against population averages.

---

# Explainability

Every progression decision should explain:

What changed.

Why.

Evidence used.

Confidence.

Example:

Bench Press increased by 2.5 kg.

Reason:

Three consecutive successful sessions.

Average RPE remained below target.

Recovery remained stable.

Confidence:

High.

---

# Learning

The Progression Engine continuously learns.

It identifies:

Fast responders.

Slow responders.

Recovery patterns.

Preferred progression rates.

Exercise-specific adaptations.

Future decisions become increasingly individualized.

---

# Interaction With Other Engines

The Progression Engine receives information from:

Recovery Engine.

Decision Engine.

Safety Engine.

Exercise Knowledge Base.

It provides updated progression targets to:

Capability Selection Engine.

Session Generation Engine.

Explainability Engine.

---

# Safety

Progression never overrides safety.

If safety recommendations conflict with progression,

safety always wins.

---

# Confidence

Every progression carries a confidence estimate.

Very High

High

Moderate

Low

Unknown

Confidence depends on evidence quality.

---

# Limitations

Progression cannot be perfectly predicted.

CAS estimates the probability that additional stimulus will improve adaptation.

It never guarantees results.

---

# Future Capabilities

Future versions may include:

Velocity-based progression.

Bar path analysis.

Strike velocity tracking.

Machine learning adaptation models.

Competition-specific progression.

Real-time wearable integration.

---

# Success Criteria

The engine succeeds when:

Performance improves.

Recovery remains sustainable.

Injury risk decreases.

Athletes remain consistent.

Progressions become increasingly individualized.

Athletes understand why progression occurred.

---

# Final Principle

Progression is not about doing more.

Progression is about becoming better.

Every increase in stimulus should be justified by evidence,

supported by recovery,

and aligned with the athlete's long-term development.